(Bare `the clock's duration`, with no unit, must keep returning whole
 seconds - this is the constraint that keeps the fix a bug fix rather than
 a language change: the bare form reaches codegen by a different route
 than the `in milliseconds` cast, and must keep its meaning. It now
 reports whole truncated seconds of the real elapsed time, so a 1500
 millisecond wait reads one, deterministically, not one-or-two depending
 on where within its starting second the timer began. Asserting equality
 guards the unit split: if the bare form ever returned milliseconds it
 would read roughly 1500, not 1.)

a timer called clock.
Start the clock.
Wait 1500 milliseconds.
Stop the clock.
a number called 'whole seconds' is the clock's duration.
If 'whole seconds' is equal to 1 then,
    Print "ok".
Otherwise,
    Print "bad".